// Records: `{x: 3, y: 4}` -- a fixed-arity product like Tuple, but each
// position also carries a field NAME, looked up by name instead of
// position. A real, separate runtime kind (Value::Record), not a
// disguised list -- see types::Type::Record's own doc comment for why:
// name-based lookup is what lets width subtyping (below) work with no
// value transformation at any boundary, and lets `.field` access (also
// below) desugar into an ordinary two-argument call with no new
// runtime opcode.
let p = {x: 3, y: 4} in

// Destructuring, the same mechanism tuples already use. A bare field
// name puns to itself: `{x, y}` in pattern position means `{x: x, y: y}`.
let sum_of_squares = match p | {x, y} -> x * x + y * y in

// `.field` access -- a peer of application in the same postfix chain
// (`f(a).x` means `(f(a)).x`), not the only way to read a record back,
// but often the more direct one.
let product = p.x * p.y in

// Width subtyping: a `{x: Int}` annotation accepts any record that has
// AT LEAST field x -- extra fields (like `y` here) just ride along,
// unobserved by anything that only ever asked for x.
let fst = fun r: {x: Int} -> r.x in

sum_of_squares + product + fst(p)
